THE CLUB COLOUR SAGA, finally understood after FIVE attempts.

  Attempts 1-4 all treated it as a CONTRAST problem: muted violet -> violet-200
  -> amber -> neutral slate surface. Each time I asked "is this readable?"

  This pass I MEASURED first: title 17:1, role 9.7:1, body 12:1, chips 14.5:1.
  Everything was far above AA. Legibility was never the complaint, so no amount
  of contrast tuning was ever going to satisfy it.

  "Find a better ASSORTMENT of colors for these boxes" = variety ACROSS the
  boxes, not a better single colour. Each org now has its own accent (cyan /
  fuchsia / amber) on the shared neutral surface.

  LESSON: when the same feedback repeats after a fix that objectively worked,
  the problem is being mis-modelled. Measure the thing you think is wrong
  before changing it again — if the measurement is fine, re-read the words.

WHEEL SCROLL SCOPE: handlers belong on the TRACK (card-width), not the wrapper
(column-width). On the wrapper, the empty space beside the cards swallowed page
scroll. Regression test: assert the wheel turns AND the page holds when over
the cards, and the inverse when beside them.

PARTICLE TEXT brightness: raising the fill colour twice did nothing perceptible
because the GAPS between dots were doing the darkening. Dot size gap-1.6 ->
gap-1 so strokes read solid. When "brighter" doesn't work, check density.

Wheel geometry rule still holds: trackHeight >= cardHeight + 2*visible*spreadX.
Current: 98 + 2*2*76 = 402.

